<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yudhishthira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yudhishthira is the eldest of the five Pandava brothers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, the son of Kunti and, in the epic, of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>. Famed for his devotion to truth and righteousness, he lost his kingdom and his brothers' freedom in a rigged game of dice, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> war he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastinapura">Hastinapura</a>. His royal sacrifice (rajasuya) is the occasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishupala">Shishupala</a>'s death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s hands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yudhishthira.jpg" alt="Yudhishthira" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nanda Lal Bose · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yudhisthira_and_His_Dog,_Ascending.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
